proposed.end_user:
Trade Surveillance Analyst (compliance analyst). They work the alert queue in the surveillance system. For each 'traded ahead of news' alert they:
- check the restricted and watch lists and who had access;
- search news with date limits to see whether public information explained the trade;
- paste links into the case file and write a short rationale;
- escalate a small share to the CCO or legal.

exa_calls:
POST /search, 'What happened after'. Body:
{query: 'MarineMax announces definitive agreement to be acquired, merger terms and price per share', type: 'fast', numResults: 5, startPublishedDate: '2026-08-06T18:30:00Z', endPublishedDate: '2026-08-12T00:00:00Z', includeDomains: ['sec.gov','investor.marinemax.com','businesswire.com','globenewswire.com','prnewswire.com'], contents: {highlights: true}}

Why: it shows the trigger event from primary sources (the Aug 10 8-K on sec.gov). fast suits a simple user-facing lookup, and the domain list is set in code. Cost: $0.007.

POST /contents (the documented fallback if call 4 is rejected, or if its synthesis cannot be shown to respect the cutoff). Body:
{urls: [top 6 URLs from call 1], snapshotAsOf: '2026-08-06T18:30:00Z', summary: {query: 'What did this page say about a MarineMax sale, bidders or an offer price?', schema: {type:'object', properties:{page_date:{type:'string'}, sale_process:{type:'string'}, bidders_named:{type:'array', items:{type:'string'}}, offer_terms:{type:'string'}}}}}

Why: Exa's Snapshot docs state that summaries are generated only from the stored version. snapshotAsOf sits at the top level on /contents. Each summary comes back as a JSON string to parse, and CONTENT_NOT_CACHED statuses are shown in the UI. The record is then assembled in code, one cited row per page. Cost: $0.001 per page.

4. Production schedule, shown as configuration plus a saved run. POST /monitors with:
  - name: '23x5 venue and clearing notices'
  - search: {query: <call 1 query>, numResults: 25, includeDomains: <the same list>, contents: {highlights: true}}
  - trigger: {type: 'interval', period: '1d'}
  - outputSchema: {type: 'object', properties: {changes: {type: 'array', items: {type: 'object', properties: {venue, title, change_type, effective_date, test_dates, action_required}}}}}. That is 7 properties in total.
  - webhook: {url: 'https://<vendor-jira-bridge>/exa', events: ['monitor.run.completed']}
Then POST /monitors/{id}/trigger runs it once on demand.
Why: daily is enough for notices published weeks ahead. The webhook must be public HTTPS, not localhost, which is why the live demo shows a saved run. Runs can be up to 30 minutes late. Monitors cost $15 per 1,000 runs.
